A code file without a header comment gets a declaration summary in the glob view instead of `(no header available)`: its first top-level declarations as their outline lines start, up to the parameter list.
- Repeated declarations, such as several `impl Arg` blocks, print once with their count (`impl Arg ×3`); members aren't summarized.
- At most five distinct declarations print, then `...` (`arg.rs`); a declaration longer than 48 characters is cut with `…` (`exports.rs`).
- Type parameters before a parameter list go with it (`pub fn parse`), and a test block keeps its title (`describe('parser')`).
- Only the first 4 KiB of a long file are read, so `...` follows what they hold (`big.rs`).
- A file whose header comment has text prints the header as before (`documented.rs`), and a file without declarations still prints `(no header available)` (`empty.rs`).
== args
input
== file input/arg.rs
pub struct Arg;

impl Arg {
    pub fn new() -> Self { Arg }
}

impl Arg {
    pub fn id(self) -> Self { self }
}

impl Clone for Arg {
    fn clone(&self) -> Self { Arg }
}

impl Arg {}

pub fn parse<T: AsRef<str>>(input: T) -> Arg { Arg }
pub(crate) fn check(arg: &Arg) {}
pub trait ArgExt {}
